In a C program, you can use the `exit()` function to terminate the program explicitly. The `exit()` function causes the current process to terminate and sends a signal to all threads. The status returned to the calling process depends on the argument passed to the function.

Here's an example of using `exit()` with an argument:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *ptr = malloc(10);

    if (ptr == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Out of memory!\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    free(ptr);
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

In this example, if the program runs out of memory, it will call `exit()` with `EXIT_FAILURE` as an argument. This will cause the program to terminate with an error status. If the program has enough memory, it will free the allocated memory and call `exit()` with `EXIT_SUCCESS`.
